1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,040
Imagine you wake up tomorrow morning and every debt on Earth is gone.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:05,600
Your mortgage? Gone.

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:07,060
Credit card? Gone.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:08,660
Student loan? Gone.

5
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:10,319
Corporate debt? Gone.

6
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:12,260
Government debt? Also gone.

7
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:15,160
Humanity has finally defeated debt.

8
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,340
Congratulations. We did it for approximately five minutes.

9
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,400
Because then banks start breaking.

10
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,710
Companies suddenly lose access to money they use to operate and expand.

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,310
Construction projects freeze.

12
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,340
Governments lose one of their biggest sources of financing.

13
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:33,680
Investors panic. And the financial system begins discovering that removing all debt is

14
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:37,680
a little like removing all the screws from an airplane because screws are technically

15
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:42,180
adding weight. The weird truth is this: the world does not simply have a lot of debt.

16
00:00:42,449 --> 00:00:44,150
The modern world runs on debt.

17
00:00:44,410 --> 00:00:47,620
And once you understand why, money starts looking completely different.

18
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,080
Let's begin with something painfully normal.

19
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,019
You want a house.

20
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,080
The house costs three hundred thousand dollars.

21
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:54,700
You check your bank account.

22
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,080
It contains not three hundred thousand dollars.

23
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,780
Fantastic. But you do have something valuable: a job,

24
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:05,070
an income, and hopefully several decades in which future you continues making money instead

25
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:06,780
of disappearing into the woods.

26
00:01:06,790 --> 00:01:10,910
So the bank gives present you three hundred thousand dollars and future you agrees to

27
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:13,290
spend the next twenty or thirty years paying it back.

28
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:14,720
That is a mortgage.

29
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:17,840
But underneath the boring paperwork is a pretty insane idea.

30
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:19,900
You're telling the bank, "I don't have the money."

31
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:21,320
The bank says, "Correct."

32
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:24,220
You say, "But twenty forty-seven me probably will."

33
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,000
And the bank says, "Good enough."

34
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:28,740
Debt allows you to bring future income into the present.

35
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,760
It is basically a time machine for money.

36
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:34,600
And once humans figured that out, we began using it for almost everything.

37
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:36,550
Businesses borrow money to build factories.

38
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:38,880
Airlines borrow money to buy planes.

39
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,960
Farmers borrow money to buy equipment.

40
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:42,600
Cities borrow money to build roads.

41
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:45,820
Governments borrow money to build infrastructure, fund wars,

42
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:49,790
survive recessions, and occasionally fund something everyone will spend the next twenty

43
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:51,000
years arguing about.

44
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:52,480
Because waiting is expensive.

45
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:56,020
Imagine you want to build a factory that costs one hundred million dollars.

46
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:58,920
Without borrowing, maybe you spend twenty years saving.

47
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:00,660
Year one, no factory.

48
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,690
Year five, still no factory.

49
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:05,220
Year twelve, very impressive spreadsheet.

50
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,460
Year twenty, congratulations.

51
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:08,820
You finally have the money.

52
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,580
Unfortunately, your competitor built three factories,

53
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:16,100
launched an app, bought your company, and renamed you Legacy Operations Division.

54
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:17,700
So instead, you borrow.

55
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:19,400
You build the factory today.

56
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,180
The factory produces goods today.

57
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:22,430
It generates revenue today.

58
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:25,040
And that future revenue hopefully repays the loan.

59
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,640
This is the first reason debt is so powerful.

60
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:30,800
Debt turns future productivity into present investment.

61
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:34,290
But now things get weird because you might imagine a bank giving someone a loan like

62
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:37,590
this. The bank walks into a giant vault, finds a pile of money,

63
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:41,160
scoops out one hundred thousand dollars and hands it to you.

64
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:43,320
Very cinematic. Very Scrooge McDuck.

65
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:45,580
Modern banking is stranger than that.

66
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:48,540
Suppose a bank approves you for a one hundred thousand dollar loan.

67
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,240
The bank records that you now owe it one hundred thousand dollars,

68
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:54,580
and then your bank account gets credited with one hundred thousand dollars.

69
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:55,960
Congratulations.

70
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,280
There is now a new deposit.

71
00:02:57,290 --> 00:03:00,750
The bank created an asset, your loan, and a liability,

72
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:04,170
your deposit. That is one reason people say banks create money when they lend,

73
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:06,500
because they do. Not infinite money.

74
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:10,060
Banks cannot simply hit copy and paste until everyone owns a Lamborghini.

75
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:13,480
They face capital rules, liquidity requirements, funding costs,

76
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:18,470
regulation, risk, and the small inconvenience that borrowers occasionally do not pay

77
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,390
them back. But lending and money creation are deeply connected,

78
00:03:21,609 --> 00:03:23,200
which creates a strange fact.

79
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:25,300
Your money is often connected to somebody else's debt.

80
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:27,580
Your bank deposit is an asset to you.

81
00:03:27,609 --> 00:03:30,090
But on another balance sheet, there is a liability.

82
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:32,680
And once you notice this, you start seeing it everywhere.

83
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:35,920
Now, here's the question that makes the entire system sound impossible.

84
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,760
If everyone is borrowing, who is lending all this money?

85
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:40,880
The answer is also everyone.

86
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:43,140
Your retirement fund might own corporate bonds.

87
00:03:43,410 --> 00:03:45,160
A bank might own government bonds.

88
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,540
An insurance company might lend to corporations.

89
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:49,800
Investment funds might finance mortgages.

90
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,460
Foreign governments may own another country's government debt.

91
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,609
Your savings may be funding somebody else's business,

92
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:57,440
and somebody else's savings may be financing your house.

93
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:01,829
The global economy is basically one enormous group chat where everyone keeps saying,

94
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:06,090
"I'll pay you later." Which is funny until you realize trillions of dollars depend on

95
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:08,120
those messages actually being true.

96
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:09,240
Take government debt.

97
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,480
Governments borrow money by selling bonds.

98
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:14,640
A bond is basically a very official IOU.

99
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,700
An investor says, "Here's money."

100
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:18,360
The government says, "Thank you.

101
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:20,680
I promise future government will deal with this."

102
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:22,480
The investor says, "Excellent."

103
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:23,980
Banks buy these I.O.U.s.

104
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,340
Pension funds buy them.

105
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:26,820
Investment funds buy them.

106
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,420
Insurance companies buy them.

107
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:30,000
Foreign governments buy them.

108
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:31,850
Even central banks hold them.

109
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:37,500
In major financial systems, high quality government bonds are not treated like some radioactive

110
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:38,830
pile of financial shame.

111
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,760
They're often considered among the safest assets available.

112
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,020
Financial institutions use them to store money.

113
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:45,520
They use them as collateral.

114
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:49,520
Their interest rates help influence the price of loans and investments across the economy.

115
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:53,020
So when you hear the government owes trillions of dollars,

116
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:57,800
your brain naturally imagines someone receiving a very aggressive credit card bill.

117
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,900
Amount due: thirty-four trillion dollars.

118
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:03,260
Minimum payment: your entire country.

119
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:06,420
But governments do not work exactly like households.

120
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,560
A household normally cannot refinance its debt forever.

121
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:11,080
Governments frequently do.

122
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:12,620
A bond matures.

123
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:13,940
The government issues another bond.

124
00:05:14,190 --> 00:05:15,380
That bond matures.

125
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:17,040
Another bond, and so on.

126
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:18,950
This is called rolling over debt.

127
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:20,650
Basically, I'll pay you Tuesday.

128
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:22,020
Tuesday arrives.

129
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:24,808
Interesting. How do you feel about next Tuesday?

130
00:05:25,128 --> 00:05:27,548
This does not mean government debt is harmless.

131
00:05:27,868 --> 00:05:30,588
If investors lose confidence, interest rates can rise.

132
00:05:30,868 --> 00:05:33,368
Higher interest costs eat into government budgets.

133
00:05:33,648 --> 00:05:35,798
Borrowing too much can become dangerous.

134
00:05:36,128 --> 00:05:40,708
Countries that borrow heavily in currencies they do not control can face even harsher

135
00:05:40,768 --> 00:05:45,308
limits. But the important question usually isn't, does this country have debt?

136
00:05:45,328 --> 00:05:47,408
Almost all modern countries do.

137
00:05:47,448 --> 00:05:51,108
The better question is, can this country handle its debt?

138
00:05:51,348 --> 00:05:56,477
Imagine two people. Person A owes ten thousand dollars and earns two hundred thousand

139
00:05:56,528 --> 00:06:01,488
dollars per year. Person B owes ten thousand dollars and earns twelve thousand dollars

140
00:06:01,508 --> 00:06:04,548
per year. Same debt, very different panic levels.

141
00:06:04,888 --> 00:06:08,588
Countries are similar. The raw number alone doesn't tell you enough.

142
00:06:08,888 --> 00:06:10,778
You care about the size of the economy,

143
00:06:11,228 --> 00:06:15,078
tax revenue, interest rates, economic growth, currency,

144
00:06:15,588 --> 00:06:19,948
when the debt needs to be repaid, who owns it, and most importantly,

145
00:06:20,008 --> 00:06:22,068
what the borrowed money was used for.

146
00:06:22,108 --> 00:06:25,848
Because debt can be productive or spectacularly stupid.

147
00:06:26,148 --> 00:06:30,388
Borrow one billion dollars to build infrastructure that makes an economy more productive

148
00:06:30,708 --> 00:06:35,188
may be excellent. Borrow one billion dollars to build a four hundred meter golden statue

149
00:06:35,228 --> 00:06:39,138
of yourself shooting lasers from its eyes, tourism might surprise us,

150
00:06:39,408 --> 00:06:42,008
but financially, we have questions.

151
00:06:42,048 --> 00:06:45,268
Debt itself is not automatically good or bad.

152
00:06:45,548 --> 00:06:50,368
Debt is a tool, and tools become dangerous when humans discover leverage.

153
00:06:50,748 --> 00:06:52,108
Leverage sounds complicated.

154
00:06:52,328 --> 00:06:56,008
It isn't. Leverage means using borrowed money to make a bigger bet.

155
00:06:56,248 --> 00:06:58,588
Suppose you have ten dollars, you invest it.

156
00:06:58,628 --> 00:07:00,308
The investment goes up ten percent.

157
00:07:00,528 --> 00:07:01,968
You make one dollar. Nice.

158
00:07:02,248 --> 00:07:03,298
Buy yourself half a coffee.

159
00:07:03,668 --> 00:07:07,448
Now, suppose you have ten dollars, but borrow another ninety.

160
00:07:07,488 --> 00:07:09,328
You invest one hundred dollars.

161
00:07:09,368 --> 00:07:11,148
The investment rises ten percent.

162
00:07:11,188 --> 00:07:12,248
You make ten dollars.

163
00:07:12,618 --> 00:07:14,908
You just doubled your original money.

164
00:07:14,968 --> 00:07:16,688
You are incredible.

165
00:07:16,728 --> 00:07:17,988
CNBC wants an interview.

166
00:07:18,288 --> 00:07:20,678
Your LinkedIn bio now says, "Investor,

167
00:07:21,168 --> 00:07:22,698
visionary, thought leader."

168
00:07:23,048 --> 00:07:24,478
You begin researching yachts.

169
00:07:24,828 --> 00:07:27,288
But then the investment falls ten percent.

170
00:07:27,348 --> 00:07:29,048
You lose ten dollars.

171
00:07:29,088 --> 00:07:30,758
Your original money, gone.

172
00:07:30,788 --> 00:07:35,108
The lender, however, would still very much like its ninety dollars.

173
00:07:35,528 --> 00:07:38,078
Suddenly, your yacht research feels premature.

174
00:07:38,548 --> 00:07:39,888
This is leverage.

175
00:07:39,948 --> 00:07:45,368
It magnifies success, and it magnifies stupidity, which explains a surprising amount

176
00:07:45,468 --> 00:07:50,388
of financial history. And now we reach the thing that can destroy this entire machine.

177
00:07:50,708 --> 00:07:53,448
Not debt, not money, not even leverage.

178
00:07:53,808 --> 00:07:56,648
Trust. Debt is a promise about the future.

179
00:07:56,968 --> 00:07:59,448
When someone lends money, they're making a prediction.

180
00:07:59,808 --> 00:08:01,528
Will this person still have a job?

181
00:08:01,868 --> 00:08:03,108
Will this company make money?

182
00:08:03,448 --> 00:08:06,708
Will this government still collect taxes twenty years from now?

183
00:08:07,068 --> 00:08:08,668
Will this property stay valuable?

184
00:08:09,008 --> 00:08:10,928
Will this factory actually work?

185
00:08:11,268 --> 00:08:14,088
If the answer looks like yes, borrowing is easier.

186
00:08:14,428 --> 00:08:18,088
If the answer looks like maybe, lenders demand more interest.

187
00:08:18,168 --> 00:08:21,558
If the answer looks like absolutely not, what are you doing?

188
00:08:21,888 --> 00:08:25,588
The money disappears, and that's where things can become nasty.

189
00:08:25,628 --> 00:08:28,008
Imagine a company begins looking risky.

190
00:08:28,088 --> 00:08:30,128
Lenders demand higher interest rates.

191
00:08:30,168 --> 00:08:32,458
Higher interest payments make the company weaker.

192
00:08:32,808 --> 00:08:36,388
That makes lenders even more nervous, so rates rise again.

193
00:08:36,688 --> 00:08:38,328
The company gets weaker again.

194
00:08:38,408 --> 00:08:42,038
Eventually, lenders stop lending entirely, and suddenly,

195
00:08:42,068 --> 00:08:46,248
a company that looked alive yesterday can run out of money today.

196
00:08:46,288 --> 00:08:48,848
This is why credit crises move so fast.

197
00:08:49,168 --> 00:08:53,128
During good times, banks lend, companies invest, consumers spend,

198
00:08:53,508 --> 00:08:54,668
asset prices rise.

199
00:08:55,008 --> 00:08:56,148
Everyone makes money.

200
00:08:56,208 --> 00:08:57,708
Everyone feels intelligent.

201
00:08:57,748 --> 00:09:01,298
Someone buys a podcast microphone, then something breaks.

202
00:09:02,008 --> 00:09:05,828
Borrowers start defaulting, lenders panic, banks become afraid to lend,

203
00:09:06,148 --> 00:09:10,428
credit disappears, companies cannot refinance, investors sell assets,

204
00:09:10,748 --> 00:09:13,268
prices fall, more borrowers get into trouble,

205
00:09:13,717 --> 00:09:18,328
and suddenly, everybody is significantly less interested in starting a podcast.

206
00:09:18,338 --> 00:09:22,168
The two thousand and eight financial crisis was one dramatic example.

207
00:09:22,308 --> 00:09:24,388
Huge amounts of mortgage debt had been created.

208
00:09:24,748 --> 00:09:27,428
Those mortgages were packaged into financial products.

209
00:09:27,808 --> 00:09:29,188
Housing prices kept rising.

210
00:09:29,508 --> 00:09:31,228
People became increasingly confident.

211
00:09:31,488 --> 00:09:33,708
Risk looked small, until it wasn't.

212
00:09:33,748 --> 00:09:35,088
Housing prices fell.

213
00:09:35,128 --> 00:09:36,468
Mortgage defaults rose.

214
00:09:36,848 --> 00:09:39,948
Financial assets tied to those mortgages lost value.

215
00:09:40,028 --> 00:09:43,508
Banks discovered that things they thought were safe were not safe.

216
00:09:43,798 --> 00:09:46,438
And because financial institutions were deeply connected,

217
00:09:46,828 --> 00:09:50,488
problems in American housing spread across global markets.

218
00:09:50,588 --> 00:09:52,388
Debt existed before the crisis.

219
00:09:52,658 --> 00:09:54,758
Debt still exists after the crisis.

220
00:09:55,088 --> 00:09:58,137
The real problem was the combination of bad lending,

221
00:09:58,528 --> 00:10:02,308
excessive leverage, weak incentives, underestimated risk,

222
00:10:02,648 --> 00:10:06,428
and far too much confidence in assumptions that turned out to be wrong.

223
00:10:06,748 --> 00:10:10,788
In other words, the system did not break because people made promises.

224
00:10:11,088 --> 00:10:15,048
It broke because too many people made promises they could not keep,

225
00:10:15,368 --> 00:10:17,308
and everyone had bet that they would.

226
00:10:17,688 --> 00:10:19,498
So now the obvious question.

227
00:10:19,528 --> 00:10:24,048
If debt can destroy banks, companies, economies, retirement accounts,

228
00:10:24,328 --> 00:10:27,908
and occasionally your uncle's property empire, why not just get rid of it?

229
00:10:28,208 --> 00:10:31,128
Because a world without debt has its own problems.

230
00:10:31,208 --> 00:10:34,428
Imagine companies can only invest money they already have.

231
00:10:34,748 --> 00:10:36,228
Factories take longer to build.

232
00:10:36,548 --> 00:10:38,268
New businesses struggle to expand.

233
00:10:38,588 --> 00:10:41,948
Infrastructure requires huge amounts of immediate taxation.

234
00:10:42,228 --> 00:10:44,428
Buying a house becomes dramatically harder.

235
00:10:44,708 --> 00:10:49,028
A twenty-five-year-old who might earn millions of dollars over their lifetime could still

236
00:10:49,088 --> 00:10:51,588
have to wait decades before buying property.

237
00:10:51,928 --> 00:10:56,088
Great entrepreneurs with little cash might never build anything.

238
00:10:56,168 --> 00:11:01,868
Meanwhile, people who already have lots of money become even more powerful because investment

239
00:11:01,938 --> 00:11:05,147
becomes much more dependent on existing wealth.

240
00:11:05,188 --> 00:11:11,038
Debt connects two groups, people who have resources today and people who believe they

241
00:11:11,048 --> 00:11:12,908
can create value tomorrow.

242
00:11:12,948 --> 00:11:15,598
At its best, credit is the bridge between them,

243
00:11:16,068 --> 00:11:19,288
and this is why debt explodes during crises.

244
00:11:19,348 --> 00:11:20,527
Imagine a recession.

245
00:11:20,548 --> 00:11:22,628
People spend less. Businesses earn less.

246
00:11:23,008 --> 00:11:24,388
Businesses fire workers.

247
00:11:24,638 --> 00:11:26,368
Those workers spend even less.

248
00:11:26,628 --> 00:11:28,408
Businesses earn even less.

249
00:11:28,768 --> 00:11:29,948
More workers get fired.

250
00:11:30,268 --> 00:11:33,848
Congratulations. We have invented the economic toilet flush.

251
00:11:34,188 --> 00:11:38,588
Governments often try to interrupt this cycle by spending on employment support,

252
00:11:38,887 --> 00:11:42,348
infrastructure, emergency programs, business assistance,

253
00:11:42,568 --> 00:11:45,968
tax relief. But recessions create an annoying problem.

254
00:11:46,458 --> 00:11:50,398
Tax revenue can fall exactly when governments need more money,

255
00:11:50,768 --> 00:11:51,788
so they borrow.

256
00:11:51,828 --> 00:11:57,318
Debt allows governments to spread crisis costs across time instead of trying to collect

257
00:11:57,388 --> 00:11:58,848
all the money immediately.

258
00:11:58,888 --> 00:12:01,808
The same logic has historically applied to wars.

259
00:12:02,188 --> 00:12:06,108
Wars are extremely expensive, and governments generally prefer,

260
00:12:06,518 --> 00:12:09,587
"We are issuing war bonds," to, "Good afternoon.

261
00:12:09,648 --> 00:12:12,398
Your taxes are now three hundred percent higher.

262
00:12:12,448 --> 00:12:16,928
Have a wonderful day." The ability to borrow has therefore been a source of state power

263
00:12:16,988 --> 00:12:22,368
for centuries. A government that people trust enough to lend to can mobilize far more

264
00:12:22,378 --> 00:12:25,238
resources than the cash sitting in its treasury today.

265
00:12:25,648 --> 00:12:27,588
Debt doesn't just shape economies.

266
00:12:27,628 --> 00:12:29,278
Debt has helped shape history.

267
00:12:29,648 --> 00:12:31,778
Which brings us back to our original question.

268
00:12:32,058 --> 00:12:34,698
The world owes an absurd amount of money.

269
00:12:35,088 --> 00:12:37,378
So who exactly do we owe it to?

270
00:12:37,768 --> 00:12:40,538
Aliens? A secret billionaire underneath Switzerland?

271
00:12:40,968 --> 00:12:43,608
One extremely patient guy named Steve?

272
00:12:44,008 --> 00:12:46,008
Mostly, we owe it to each other.

273
00:12:46,348 --> 00:12:47,388
Households owe banks.

274
00:12:47,768 --> 00:12:49,048
Companies owe investors.

275
00:12:49,348 --> 00:12:53,588
Governments owe banks, pension funds, citizens, and other governments.

276
00:12:53,908 --> 00:12:55,328
Banks owe depositors.

277
00:12:55,668 --> 00:12:57,748
Financial institutions owe one another.

278
00:12:58,128 --> 00:13:01,088
Your asset is often somebody else's liability.

279
00:13:01,448 --> 00:13:06,908
Your savings can fund somebody else's future, and their savings can fund yours.

280
00:13:06,948 --> 00:13:11,148
The global financial system is not really a giant pile of cash.

281
00:13:11,188 --> 00:13:13,928
It is a gigantic network of promises.

282
00:13:14,248 --> 00:13:16,208
I'll pay you next month. I'll pay you next year.

283
00:13:16,508 --> 00:13:18,228
I'll pay you in thirty years.

284
00:13:18,708 --> 00:13:24,708
Debt allows savers to give up spending today so borrowers can spend or invest today in

285
00:13:24,748 --> 00:13:27,178
exchange for being repaid from tomorrow.

286
00:13:27,548 --> 00:13:29,478
Interest is the price of waiting.

287
00:13:29,908 --> 00:13:32,788
Risk determines how expensive that waiting becomes.

288
00:13:33,098 --> 00:13:37,668
And the entire system ultimately depends on one gigantic assumption:

289
00:13:38,008 --> 00:13:42,368
tomorrow will be productive enough to pay for what we build today.

290
00:13:42,738 --> 00:13:44,208
When that assumption is correct,

291
00:13:44,657 --> 00:13:48,048
debt can help economies grow dramatically faster.

292
00:13:48,088 --> 00:13:51,228
When it is wrong, things become interesting.

293
00:13:51,268 --> 00:13:53,708
So yes, the world runs on money.

294
00:13:53,728 --> 00:13:56,828
But money itself runs on something even stranger:

295
00:13:57,248 --> 00:14:03,068
trust, expectations, promises about the future, and perhaps more than anything else,

296
00:14:03,548 --> 00:14:05,768
I.O.U.s.

297
00:14:06,168 --> 00:14:08,918
So if every debt on Earth disappeared tomorrow,

298
00:14:09,348 --> 00:14:10,308
you might celebrate.

299
00:14:10,608 --> 00:14:12,948
Just don't celebrate for more than five minutes.
